  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a surface trough \nmoving east across the area this morning, accompanied by mainly \nrain showers, though occasional wet snow may mix in at times. \nWater vapor imagery also depicts a narrow ridge approaching the \narea from the west which will result in dry weather today with \nperiods of mid-level cloud cover, albeit slightly below normal \ntemperatures with highs in the low to mid-40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA sharp upper-level trough will swing east through the Great \nLakes on Sunday into Monday, bringing the next chance for \nwidespread precipitation. At the surface, a compact low will \ndevelop and move east across the Lower Great Lakes, extending a \ncold front into the area and briefly ushering in a colder air \nmass on Monday. Will continue to monitor trends in the wind \nfield with this system as cold air advection could result in \nsome gustier winds (35 to 45 mph) translating down to the \nsurface, especially Sunday night into Monday morning. Rainfall \namounts have slightly trended higher with this system, generally\nranging between a half and three quarters of an inch with \nlocally higher amounts up to an inch possible. Some wet snow may\nmix in behind the front Monday morning, though not anticipating\nany accumulations as surface high pressure and upper- level \nridging quickly arrive into the region from the west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nUpper-level ridging will gradually build across the Eastern \nCONUS, resulting in temperatures rising above average into the \n50s on Wednesday and perhaps even near 60 by Friday. Another \nupper-level trough will impact the region towards the end of the\nweek or the early part of the weekend, though early indicators \nsuggest this to be a warmer system with model guidance largely \nin favor of a split flow upper-level regime, keeping any colder \nair confined further north near Hudson Bay. Another round of \nwidespread rain is expected with this system.",
